  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 153 PM MDT Mon Aug 31 2026\n\nThe subtropical ridge continues its crawl to the east and now\nbarely extends into eastern New Mexico, as upper level troughing\nover the west coast continues to edge in. As expected, several\nrounds of showers and thunderstorms have moved up across Arizona\ninto the Four Corners region overnight and this morning. The\ntemperature contrast from west to east has also set up a steady\nW to WNW wind in southwestern New Mexico. This has advected in \ndeeper low level moisture, and dewpoints in the 60s can still be\nfound across the San Simon and Animas Valley. 50s are common\nthrough Luna and western Sierra Counties, and east of El Paso,\nwith 40s around the Mesilla Valley and Tularosa Basin.\n\nExpect convection to continue to build over the high terrain\nthrough this afternoon, favoring western areas initially. Isolated\nthunderstorms will develop over desert mountain ranges later this\nafternoon, independent of outflow boundary interactions, helped\nalong by weak low level convergence associated with a thermal\ntrough along the Rio Grande. By evening outflow from the west will\nhelp enhance the low level convergence, with thunderstorm coverage\nincreasing to scattered in the south-central lowlands, including\naround Las Cruces, and potentially in or near El Paso as well. \n\nLocalized flooding can't be completely ruled out in parts of the\nGila and Bootheel Regions, where south-to-north echo training may\noccur. Rain will be steadiest in the Bootheel, where PWAT values\nare hovering around 1.25 inches. This areas will also be closest\nto weak shortwave troughs riding up in SSW flow near the Arizona\nborder, loosely associated with a sharper trough located over\nsouthern Nevada.\n\nThe overnight hours are a little less certain, as some high res\nmodels suggest nocturnal showers and storms continuing in the\nsouth-central lowlands through about midnight. This appears to be\ndue to increasing low level convergence along the Rio Grande\n(aided by outflow boundaries), coupled with weak shortwave \ntroughs continuing to graze the area, all located under a broad \nregion of upper level diffluent flow high aloft. \n\nFor Tuesday, the main trough in Nevada will lift into Wyoming,\nwith weakening upper level support in New Mexico. Upper level\ndivergence shifts to eastern New Mexico, but a surface trough will\nstill linger east of the Rio Grande. The HRRR is particularly\noptimistic about precip chances near and east of the Rio Grande\nTuesday afternoon, with sharply lower chances in southwestern New\nMexico (further away from the upper level support, and likely\nworked-over in terms of instability after today).\n\nThe western limb of the ridge tries to rebuild in Wednesday,\nreducing precip coverage, especially east of the Rio Grande,\nthough some moisture will begin working into SW New Mexico again.\nPersistent western troughing looks to nudge into the ridge late \nin the week, increasing overall moisture (PWAT 1.2-1.4 inches) and\nfavoring an uptick in thunderstorms again through at least\nSaturday.\n\nTemperatures will remain a little above normal, but we look to\nstay in the mid to upper-90s for most of the lowlands after \ntoday.",
